{"item_id":"E-OPT-417","title":"When spot checks stop finding the problem","body":"Spot checks work while a business is small enough that one person can still catch the same mistakes by eye. They stop working when you keep checking samples and the same errors still repeat, or when new ones slip through between checks. At that point, the real question is no longer “should we inspect more?” It is “what should be checked automatically, and what must stay human?”

For a small business, the best first candidate is a repeatable document or data review with clear rules. Think invoice amounts against an expected limit, customer replies against a simple FAQ, or recurring form fields that should match a known pattern. The point is not to replace judgment. It is to make routine flagging reliable so the owner only reviews exceptions. Money movement and other high-risk decisions still need human approval.

A useful next step is to name the one check that fails most often and ask whether it fails because the rule is unclear, the input changes too much, or the review is simply too manual to keep up. If the answer is still vague, that is usually the boundary where spot checks have stopped being enough."}

